From 824b8e39303443e872f2fef0bd1af1e07528313c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lance <Gero3977@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 14:25:19 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 19/22] iio: qcom-smgr: wait up to a second for a one-shot
 sample

A one-shot sysfs read starts the sensor, waits for the first report and
stops it again. The wait was 250 ms. On an LG V30 the gyroscope missed
that about once in eight reads when reads were spaced apart (1 of 8),
and never when they ran back to back (0 of 6), so a sensor that has gone
idle can need longer to produce its first sample. The read then failed
with -ETIMEDOUT even though the sensor was fine.

Wait up to a second. This only changes how long a read blocks when the
sample is late; reads that get a sample return as soon as it arrives.

The actual start-up delay has not been measured, so whether one second
removes every timeout still has to be confirmed by repeated reads.

Signed-off-by: Lance <Gero3977@gmail.com>
Assisted-by: Claude-Code:claude-opus-5-5
---
 drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c b/drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c
index 38b6ff5d7..8b123603a 100644
--- a/drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c
+++ b/drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c
@@ -411,7 +411,7 @@ static int qcom_smgr_iio_read_raw(struct iio_dev *iio_dev,
 				return ret;
 			}
 			ret = wait_for_completion_timeout(&sensor->sample_ready,
-				msecs_to_jiffies(250));
+				msecs_to_jiffies(1000));
 			qcom_smgr_request_buffering(smgr, sensor, false);
 			mutex_unlock(&sensor->oneshot_lock);
 			if (!ret)
